WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.240
The program carries one rule
forward unchanged: Critical and High

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.725
misconfigurations are fixed and verified
before any certificate workload migrates.

3
00:00:09.725 --> 00:00:12.833
Migrating from a CA that
can be tricked into issuing

4
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:16.375
arbitrary credentials just
carries the weakness forward.

5
00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:20.950
And under the bring-your-own-CA model
the program recommends, the new cloud

6
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.975
issuer chains to the existing hierarchy,
so it inherits every weakness on day one.

7
00:00:25.975 --> 00:00:30.932
The ESC labels from SpecterOps' 2021
research are the shared vocabulary,

8
00:00:30.932 --> 00:00:34.237
and the book uses them
only for defensive checks.

9
00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:38.800
They live in four places: templates,
the forest's PKI objects,

10
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.712
the CA's own settings, and
web enrollment endpoints.

11
00:00:42.712 --> 00:00:46.687
Put a Defender for Identity
sensor on every CA that stays.

12
00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:52.200
In GCC, some of these assessments are still
preview, so run the manual checks too.
